From 236ff2538d2cdb6ff8a59a75284b49205f0fc442 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 12:58:31 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 32/44] feat(chexc): package metadata records what a package's
 capability lists are worth

A consumer could not tell a verified list from a trusted one: the '.cjo' recorded nothing about
how the package was checked. It now carries three facts -- the severity capability checking ran
at, whether effect handlers were enabled, and whether the package carries capability metadata at
all, which the first two cannot express for a '.cjo' written before the feature. All three are
additive fields with defaults, so an old '.cjo' still loads and reports exactly that: nothing
recorded.

Two rules follow immediately. An '@AssumeThrows' on a package verified at 'error' is rejected --
an assumption stands in for lists a dependency does not have, and where it has proven ones the
two contradict each other; at 'warn' the lists are knowingly incomplete, which is when an
assumption is still the tool for the job, so that stays legal. And a package compiled with effect
handlers but no capability metadata is rejected rather than read as empty: its 'performs' lists
were never written, so an empty one means "unknown", not "performs nothing".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
